! C22-6578 PROBLEM 2.5, "REMOVAL OF KEY WORDS", printed p.16 (PDF 17),
! read in the ink.  The one printed method, as printed and corrected.
!
!   "Given a string of 100 6-bit bytes beginning at DATA, remove any 4
!   consecutive bytes which match a given "key word" KEY.  Pack the
!   result starting at ANSW."
!
! THE PRINTED STATEMENTS are block 1 below, as printed, except that its
! BEW, $ is replaced by "B, BLK2".  DATA, KEY and ANSW's
! initial contents are the deck's; the page gives none.
!
! >>> THE PRINTED PROGRAM STORES ONE BYTE TOO MANY PER KEY FOUND. <<<
! Worked by hand from the printed statements:
!
!   $1's count starts at 100-3 = 97, the number of 4-byte windows.  A
!   window that does not match stores one byte and CB takes one from
!   the count.  A window that DOES match removes four windows from the
!   string (V+, $1, X18 skips the other three bytes) but the count
!   loses only three -- C-I, $1, 3 -- because the printed "B, LODE"
!   goes round the CB.  So each key found leaves the count one too
!   high, the loop loads one window past the end of DATA, and ANSW
!   gets one byte of whatever follows DATA.
!
!   The label CAB is on the CB and NOTHING ON THE PAGE REFERS TO IT.
!   "B, CAB" in place of "B, LODE" makes the count right, and is the
!   natural reading of why the label is there.  That is an inference;
!   the ink says B, LODE (read at the page's own resolution, clear).
!
! Block 1 is AS PRINTED; block 2 is the same with B, CAB2, NOT PRINTED.
! Both have a second hazard the deck does not run: a key found in the
! last four windows takes the count through zero and the loop runs on.
!
! THE DATA.  Byte i of DATA is (7i+3) mod 64, i = 0..99, except that
! the key, octal 61 62 63 01, is laid at bytes 10-13 and 50-53.  No
! other four bytes match.  DATA is followed by bytes 52 25 52 25 ...
! (octal), so what the printed program picks up past the end is known.
!
! EXPECTED VALUES ARE HAND-DERIVED, not taken from the model: the
! statement's answer by deleting the two keys from the byte list, and
! the printed program's by tracing its ten statements (the arithmetic
! above; both were done with a ten-line trace on the host that knows
! nothing of the model).
!
!   Statement: 92 bytes, 552 bits, ending 24 bits into the ninth word.
!   Block 2 (B, CAB): exactly that -- ninth word E55C8EAC78 FFFFFF, the
!     six F's being ANSW2's untouched fill.  91 loads.
!   Block 1 (as printed): the same 92 bytes and then TWO more, octal
!     52 25, the first two bytes after DATA: ninth word E55C8EAC78 A95
!     FFF.  93 loads.  ANS1_w8 pins this; it is what the printed
!     statements do, not what the problem asks.
!
! Reference Manual printed p.21 for progressive indexing.  The final
! "ST(BU,18), 0($2)" is ordinary indexing of a zero address by $2.
!
! MEASURED, against a control of 21 of 21 (ten words of each answer and
! the halt PC):
!  * KEY's last byte 01 -> 02, so nothing matches (C72CC1 -> C72CC2).
!    OBSERVED 20 fail, every word of both answers; the PC passes.  Both
!    blocks then copy DATA's 100 bytes unchanged -- words 0-8 are DATA's
!    own nine words and word 9 is 8EAC78 and forty untouched 1s, 600
!    bits -- which is what the statement predicts with no key present,
!    and both blocks agree, as they must when the AE path never runs.
!
! The printed ' comments are written !' here: this assembler's comment
! mark is the exclamation mark.

        SLC, 1000.0
! --- 1. AS PRINTED -------------------------------------------------
START   LX, $1, XW1; LX, $2, XW2
LODE    L(BU, 24) (V+I), 0.6($1)
        K(BU,24), KEY
        BAE, AE
        ST(BU,6) (V+I), 0.6($2), 18
CAB     CB, $1, LODE
        ST(BU,18), 0($2)                !'store remaining 3 bytes
        B, BLK2
AE      V+, $1, X18                     !'skip 3 more bytes
        C-I, $1, 3                      !'3 means 3.0 here
        B, LODE
XW1     XW, DATA, 100-3, $
XW2     XW, ANSW, 0, $
X18     VF, 0.18
! --- 2. B, CAB instead of B, LODE.  NOT PRINTED. --------------------
BLK2    LX, $1, XW1; LX, $2, XW2B
LODE2   L(BU, 24) (V+I), 0.6($1)
        K(BU,24), KEY
        BAE, AE2
        ST(BU,6) (V+I), 0.6($2), 18
CAB2    CB, $1, LODE2
        ST(BU,18), 0($2)
HALT    BEW, $
AE2     V+, $1, X18
        C-I, $1, 3
        B, CAB2
XW2B    XW, ANSW2, 0, $

        SLC, 1200.0
DATA    DD(BU,64,8), (16)0CA4587E6B74EC2C
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)72CC196CCFA0483D
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)6764AF2E401CE55C
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)8EAC78FC63546E2A
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)70DFE14C4DAC72CC
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)1F442D26609EED7C
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)0CA4587E6B74EC22
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)505DE96CCFA0483D
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)6764AF2E401CE55C
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)8EAC78A95A95A95A
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)9500000000000000
KEY     DD(BU,64,8), (16)C72CC10000000000

        SLC, 1220.0
ANSW    DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

        SLC, 1240.0
ANSW2   DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF
        END, START
